                <pb facs="0790" n="678" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg678"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/12967: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-20T10:00:00+08:00">February 20, 1935—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 20—3:03 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>83. Reference Legation’s 74, February 13, 1 p.m.,<anchor n="85" xml:id="d663-f1"
                    /> following telegram has been received from the Consul General at Hankow:
                        <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“February 19, 6 p.m. Reliable information Reds from northern Szechwan
                            have crossed border into Shensi with the probable intent to proceed
                            westward to Kansu and Sinkiang. Letters to China Inland Mission from
                            Fenghsien, Shensi, dated February 13th, state that Ningkiang, Mienhsien
                            and Lioyang in southwest Shensi taken by Reds. No Americans stationed at
                            places. Have warned Americans Shensi, Kansu, Chinghai and Sinkiang by
                            letter and also sent telegrams warning to Fenghsien, Shensi and
                            Tienshui, Minhsien and Tsingshui, Kansu.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
